\section{Introduction}

Poker is a typical example of a stochastic, imperfect-information game that presents substantial hurdles to AI research.  Unlike deterministic games like chess or Go, poker necessitates reasoning under ambiguity, bluffing, and strategic adaptation to opponents' actions.  The creation of AI bots capable of playing poker optimally has thus become a benchmark problem in computational game theory and decision-making research.

In recent years, breakthroughs in search algorithms and probabilistic modelling methods have allowed AI programs to outperform humans in a various types of poker variants.   Counterfactual regret minimisation (CFR) \cite{zinkevich_cfr}, Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) \cite{mcts_poker}, and deep learning-based techniques \cite{deepstack2017,libratus2017} have been successfully applied in both heads-up and multiplayer games.   These methods allow the agents to test and examine large state spaces, simulate potential outcomes, and make effective intelligent decisions in real time very efficiently.

Our Paper describes the details of structural design and implementation of a  Texas Hold'em poker AI that uses the Expectiminimax algorithm and Monte Carlo simulation to access strength of hand and choose the moves that are best.  This system is benchmarked in a various types of  of scenarios, including AI vs deterministic rule-based opponents simulation, AI vs AI simulation, and AI versus human players simulation.  The initial contributions of this paper's works are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A poker playing setting capable of handling a various types of opponents.
    \item The integration of Expectiminimax search with Monte Carlo hand evaluation to manage uncertainty in not perfect information games.
    \item Performance evaluation demonstrating the efficacy of the proposed AI methods in variety of  gaming scenarios.
\end{enumerate}

The prompt of the paper is figured as follows: One Section provides a review of related papers and their work and methodologies. One Section  describes the methodology,  game state representation, and algorithms used in the work. One Section represents the experimental setup and results ( tables). One Section  added conclusion to the paper and discusses future improvements.
